{"maxId":246,"entries":{"chapter-45-study-syncope-and-dizziness|Chapter 45 Study: Syncope and Dizziness":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Syncope: transient loss of consciousness 2° cerebral hypoperfusion, with unresponsiveness/loss of postural tone and spontaneous, complete recovery (p665)":["u3"],"copy|Dizziness: abnormal perception of the body's relationship to space; 4 subtypes — vertigo, presyncope, disequilibrium, and other (p665, p670)":["u4"],"copy|Syncope lifetime prevalence up to 47% in healthy adults; 3% of ED visits (p665)":["u5"],"copy|Incidence 0.6%/yr, rising to 2% to 6%/yr in older adults (p665)":["u6"],"copy|1-yr outcomes after ED syncope visit: 7% mortality, 16% recurrence needing hospitalization, 6.0% device insertion (p665)":["u7"],"copy|Syncope = 7th most common reason for ED admission in patients >65; 1% of all hospital admissions; costs the US $2.4 billion/yr; up to 40% remain unexplained despite inpatient workup (p666)":["u8"],"copy|Young adults: peak incidence age 10–30, mostly vasovagal. Older adults: more often cardiovascular (p666)":["u9"],"copy|Dizziness prevalence 4% to 30% in persons ≥65, higher in females (p666)":["u10"],"copy|Cardiac causes responsible for about one-third of syncope cases in older patients (p667)":["u11"],"copy|Table 45-1 causes of syncope: cardiac = structural heart disease, myocardial dysfunction, or arrhythmia (bradyarrhythmia incl. AV block, or tachyarrhythmia); noncardiac = neurologic, neurally mediated, respiratory, or vascular (p667)":["u12"],"copy|Standing pools 500 to 1000 cc of blood in legs/splanchnic bed → ↓ venous return → baroreflex response (increased sympathetic outflow, decreased vagal tone) limits the BP fall (p666)":["u13"],"copy|Aging blunts this defense: ↓ baroreflex sensitivity, defective β-adrenergic signal transduction → diminished HR response to standing/exercise (p666)":["u14"],"copy|Older adults also lose blood volume: ↓ renin/aldosterone, ↑ atrial natriuretic peptide, ↓ thirst response, often plus diuretics (p666)":["u15"],"copy|OH definition: ≥20 mmHg systolic and/or ≥10 mmHg diastolic drop on standing, usually measured immediately and after a short delay (eg, at 1 and 3 minutes) (p667)":["u16"],"copy|OH-contributing drug classes: antipsychotics (MAOIs, TCAs), diuretics, beta-blockers, nitrates/alpha-blockers — alpha+beta combination especially dangerous (p668)":["u17"],"copy|PPH definition: ≥20 mmHg systolic drop within 2 hours of starting a meal (p668)":["u18"],"copy|PPH prevalence: 24% to 36% of nursing home residents; 23% of older adults admitted to a geriatric hospital with syncope/falls; found in 50% of older adults with unexplained syncope (p668)":["u19"],"copy|CSH definition: asystole ≥3 sec (cardioinhibitory) and/or systolic BP fall >50 mmHg (vasodepressor) during carotid sinus massage (p669)":["u20"],"copy|Massage technique: done sequentially on each side; recommended duration 5 to 10 sec; maximal HR fall occurs within 5 sec of onset (p669)":["u21"],"copy|CSS = CSH plus syncope on stimulation; vasovagal syncope or OH coexists in one-third of CSH patients; about half of CSS patients sustain an injury/fracture during episodes (p669)":["u22"],"copy|Presence of heart disease independently predicts cardiac syncope: sensitivity 95%, specificity 45% (p671)":["u23"],"copy|BNP ≥300 pg/mL or NT-proBNP >156 pg/mL independently associated with major adverse cardiovascular events (MACE) after ED syncope evaluation (p671)":["u24"],"copy|Cardiac arrhythmias evident in up to 50% of patients with EF <40%; atrial fibrillation in 1 in 5 men older than 80 (p671)":["u25"],"copy|24-hour ambulatory ECG/Holter: diagnostic yield only 1% to 2% in unselected populations (p671–672)":["u26"],"copy|In a review of 2106 patients ≥65 admitted after syncope, postural BP was measured in only 38% of episodes, yet had the highest diagnostic yield of any test: affected diagnosis 18%–26%, management 25%–30%, and determined etiology 15%–21% (p672)":["u27"],"copy|~10% of ED syncope patients have a serious outcome within 7 to 30 days of the visit (p673)":["u28"],"copy|Dix-Hallpike maneuver (Figure 45-1) BPPV criteria: latency 1 to 5 sec between maneuver completion and onset, nystagmus/vertigo lasting 10–20 sec, and fatigability (decreasing intensity) with repeated testing (p673–674)":["u29"],"copy|Episode duration is a clue. BPPV typically causes positional attacks lasting less than 1 minute. Spontaneous attacks can overlap in TIA, vestibular migraine and Ménière disease. Definite Ménière disease includes attacks lasting 20 minutes–12 hours with characteristic auditory findings; probable episodes can last up to 24 hours (p670; Bárány Society criteria).":["u30"],"copy|Vestibular rehabilitation: improvement usually seen after 6 to 8 weeks (p676)":["u31"],"copy|Epley canalith repositioning maneuver (Figure 45-2): each of the 5 head positions held 10 to 15 sec or until vertigo subsides (p676)":["u32"],"copy|Cardioinhibitory CSS: dual-chamber cardiac pacing abolishes syncope in 85% to 90% of patients (p675)":["u33"],"copy|Syncope caused by sustained VT warrants electrophysiologic assessment for an ICD, including ventricular function and reversible causes. After a previous MI, unexplained syncope with inducible sustained VT on electrophysiologic study is an ICD indication. Previous MI alone does not justify implantation (p675; ESC syncope guideline).":["u34"],"copy|For severe symptomatic OH, specialist options include midodrine 2.5–10 mg up to three times daily while awake (avoid near bedtime) or fludrocortisone 0.05–0.2 mg daily with potassium, edema and supine BP monitoring. Avoid fludrocortisone in heart failure. For postprandial hypotension, the chapter discusses caffeine 250 mg in the morning and a small study of subcutaneous octreotide 50 micrograms before a glucose load (p675; OH review, AHA statement).Octreotide was studied in micrograms. The textbook’s milligram unit overstates the trial dose by a factor of 1,000 (Jansen et al., 1989).":["u35"],"recall|Recall":["u36"],"copy|CHAPTER 45s / HAZZARD":["u37"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 45 — Syncope and Dizziness; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u38"],"copy|Definitions":["u39"],"copy|Epidemiology":["u40"],"copy|Pathophysiology":["u41"],"copy|Orthostatic and postprandial hypotension":["u42"],"copy|Carotid sinus hypersensitivity / carotid sinus syndrome (CSS)":["u43"],"copy|Evaluation of syncope":["u44"],"copy|Management":["u45"],"copy|1.":["u46"],"copy|What is the definition of syncope, and how does it differ from a seizure or TIA?":["u47"],"copy|Transient loss of consciousness from cerebral hypoperfusion, with loss of postural tone and spontaneous, complete recovery.":["u48"],"copy|Other transient causes of LOC (eg, vertebrobasilar ischemia, hypoxemia, hypoglycemia) are technically not syncope if they involve focal neurologic abnormalities or do not recover spontaneously without intervention (eg, oxygen or glucose).":["u49"],"copy|Seizures may occur during a syncopal event as a consequence of cerebral hypoperfusion, but are rarely a cause of syncope (p665).":["u50"],"copy|2.":["u51"],"copy|What are the 4 subtypes of dizziness, and how do their typical time courses differ?":["u52"],"copy|Vertigo, presyncope, disequilibrium, and other.":["u53"],"copy|Vertigo and presyncope are typically acute/episodic; disequilibrium and \"other\" are usually chronic and sustained (p665).":["u54"],"copy|3.":["u55"],"copy|What proportion of syncope cases remain unexplained despite extensive inpatient evaluation, and what does syncope cost the US annually?":["u56"],"copy|Up to 40% remain unexplained; syncope-related hospitalizations have been estimated to cost the US $2.4 billion annually (p666).":["u57"],"copy|4.":["u58"],"copy|What blood pressure criteria define orthostatic hypotension (OH), and at what time points should BP be measured?":["u59"],"copy|A decline of ≥20 mmHg systolic and/or ≥10 mmHg diastolic when moving from supine/sitting to standing, measured immediately and again after a short delay (typically 1 and 3 minutes) (p667).":["u60"],"copy|5.":["u61"],"copy|What defines postprandial hypotension (PPH), and what fraction of nursing home residents have it?":["u62"],"copy|A decline of ≥20 mmHg systolic BP within 2 hours of starting a meal.":["u63"],"copy|Prevalence is 24% to 36% in nursing home residents (p668).":["u64"],"copy|6.":["u65"],"copy|What are the objective criteria for carotid sinus hypersensitivity (CSH) on carotid sinus massage?":["u66"],"copy|Asystole ≥3 seconds (cardioinhibitory) and/or a fall in systolic BP >50 mmHg (vasodepressor), or a combination of both (mixed), during carotid sinus massage (p669).":["u67"],"copy|7.":["u68"],"copy|How long should carotid sinus massage be performed, and when does the maximal heart-rate fall occur?":["u69"],"copy|5 to 10 seconds (massage is done sequentially on each side); the maximal fall in heart rate usually occurs within 5 seconds of the onset of massage (p669).":["u70"],"copy|8.":["u71"],"copy|In a large review of hospitalized syncope patients, how often were postural BP measurements actually performed, and how useful were they when done?":["u72"],"copy|Only in 38% of episodes — yet they had the highest diagnostic yield of any test, affecting diagnosis in 18%–26%, management in 25%–30%, and determining etiology in 15%–21% of cases (p672).":["u73"],"copy|9.":["u74"],"copy|What are the diagnostic criteria for BPPV on the Dix-Hallpike maneuver?":["u75"],"copy|(1) Vertigo with rotatory nystagmus; (2) latency of 1–5 seconds between completing the maneuver and onset of symptoms; (3) paroxysmal nystagmus/vertigo lasting 10–20 seconds; and (4) fatigability — decreasing intensity with repeated testing (p673–674).":["u76"],"copy|10.":["u77"],"copy|How does the time course of episodic dizziness help distinguish BPPV, TIA/migraine, and Meniere disease?":["u78"],"copy|BPPV commonly causes positional spells under 1 minute.":["u79"],"copy|TIA and vestibular migraine can overlap in duration.":["u79"],"copy|Definite Ménière attacks last 20 minutes–12 hours with characteristic auditory findings, and probable attacks can last up to 24 hours (p670; Bárány Society criteria, PMID 25882471).":["u79"],"copy|11.":["u80"],"copy|What sensitivity and specificity does the presence of heart disease carry for predicting a cardiac cause of syncope?":["u81"],"copy|Sensitivity 95%, specificity 45% (p671).":["u82"],"copy|12.":["u83"],"copy|What is the diagnostic yield of a 24-hour ambulatory Holter monitor in an unselected syncope population, and what device is replacing it?":["u84"],"copy|Only 1% to 2% diagnostic yield in unselected populations; implantable loop recorders (ILRs), with higher diagnostic yield, are increasingly recommended as an early diagnostic tool, especially in older patients (p671–672).":["u85"],"copy|15.":["u86"],"copy|What proportion of ED syncope patients suffer a serious outcome within 7–30 days, and name three high-risk features that warrant hospital admission.":["u87"],"copy|About 10%.":["u88"],"copy|High-risk features include persistently abnormal vital signs, GI bleed or acute change in hematocrit to <30, volume depletion, acute coronary syndrome, implantable cardiac device dysfunction, valvular heart disease, new undiagnosed murmur, family history of sudden death, new dyspnea/CHF features, prolonged QT or other conduction disease, slow AF (<40 bpm), persistent bradycardia (<40 bpm), Brugada pattern on ECG, or syncope during exertion/supine/sitting (p673 — any three of these).":["u89"],"copy|Answer":["u90","u91","u92","u93","u94","u95","u96","u97","u98","u99","u100","u101","u102"],"copy|Drugs that can contribute to OH include antipsychotics, antidepressants such as MAOIs and TCAs, diuretics, beta-blockers, nitrates and alpha-blockers. Combined alpha and beta blockade further impairs compensation on standing (p668)":["u17"],"copy|CSS requires an abnormal response that reproduces the patient’s usual spontaneous symptoms in a compatible clinical history; vasovagal syncope or OH coexists in one-third of CSH patients; about half of CSS patients sustain an injury/fracture during episodes (p669)":["u22"],"copy|Brief myoclonic jerks can occur during syncope from hypoperfusion.":["u50"],"copy|They do not, by themselves, establish an epileptic seizure (p665).":["u50"],"copy|What is the diagnostic yield of a 24-hour ambulatory Holter monitor in an unselected syncope population, and when can longer monitoring help?":["u84"],"copy|Only 1% to 2% diagnostic yield in unselected populations; a Holter is useful when symptoms are frequent.":["u85"],"copy|An implantable loop recorder can capture an infrequent recurrent episode when arrhythmia remains a concern after the initial assessment (p671–672).":["u85"],"copy|The chapter calls an abnormal massage response CSH and uses CSS when carotid sinus stimulation produces syncope; vasovagal syncope or OH coexists in one-third of CSH patients; about half of CSS patients sustain an injury/fracture during episodes (p669)":["u22"],"copy|Episode duration. Spells under 1 minute suggest BPPV, 20–120 minutes suggests TIA or migraine, and over 120 minutes through 2 days suggests Ménière disease or recurrent vestibulopathy (p670). Current practice (not the book): The Bárány criteria use 20 minutes–12 hours for definite Ménière disease and up to 24 hours for probable disease, with accompanying auditory criteria. Bárány Society, 2015.":["u30"],"copy|On p675 the chapter lists ICD indications as syncope due to VT with EF <35%, syncope with previous MI, and VT induced during electrophysiologic testing. It also discusses an ICD for recurrent VT syncope with EF >35% after drug therapy and ablation fail. Current practice (not the book): After prior MI, unexplained syncope with inducible sustained VT on electrophysiologic study is an ICD indication. A history of MI alone does not establish this indication. ESC syncope guideline, 2018.":["u34"],"copy|For OH or postprandial hypotension, p675 lists morning caffeine 250 mg, oral fludrocortisone 0.1–0.5 mg daily, oral midodrine 2.5–10 mg three times daily, and subcutaneous octreotide 30 minutes before a meal. The printed octreotide dose is 50 mg [sic — the cited study used 50 micrograms, not milligrams]. Jansen et al., 1989. Current practice (not the book): When drug treatment is appropriate, fludrocortisone is generally dosed at 0.05–0.2 mg/day. Monitor potassium, edema and supine blood pressure and avoid it in heart failure. Avoid midodrine near bedtime. OH review, 2022 · AHA scientific statement, 2024.":["u243"],"copy|The chapter describes sleeping with the head raised 30–45 degrees as potentially helpful, the September paper’s listed intervention (pp.":["u147"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): How to interpret the angle.":["u149"],"copy|For symptomatic cardioinhibitory carotid sinus syndrome, the book calls dual-chamber pacing the treatment of choice (pp669, 672, 675).":["u244"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): Dual-chamber pacing can be used in selected patients over 40 with severe recurrent unpredictable syncope and a documented cardioinhibitory response.":["u194"],"copy|A vasodepressor component can persist.":["u195"],"copy|After the maneuver, the book advises avoiding lying flat for 24–48 hours, or using a cervical collar (pp.":["u203"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): Routine post-maneuver positional restrictions and routine vestibular suppressants are discouraged.":["u245"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): Continuous acute vertigo needs a stroke assessment.":["u212"],"copy|They do not, by themselves, establish an epileptic seizure (pp665, 669).":["u50"],"copy|On p670, positional spells under 1 minute suggest BPPV, spells lasting 20–120 minutes suggest TIA or migraine, and spells lasting over 120 minutes through 2 days suggest Ménière disease or recurrent vestibulopathy.":["u246"]},"sequenceOffsets":[[247,-144],[291,-143],[292,-142],[336,-139],[342,-138],[350,-137],[380,0]]}
